/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Let x[n] be the sample at time index n, x̂[n] its prediction from earlier samples and x̃[n] the reconstructed sample that both ends can compute.
   - The reconstructed sample differs from the input only by the quantization error of the difference: x[n] − x̃[n] = e[n] − q[n].
   - The simplest predictor uses the last reconstructed sample, x̂[n] = x̃[n − 1].
   - The figure shows the transmitter and the receiver separately.
   - Because both predictors see the same x̃, they stay identical, and the error in each output sample is just one quantization error, e[n] − q[n].
   - Gp = 10 log10(σ²/σe²) dB
   - Gp is the prediction gain: the SQNR improvement over PCM with the same number of bits, since the quantizer now covers a range set by σe, not σ.
   - The gain is not automatic.
   - ADPCM makes the quantizer step Δ[n] follow the short-term level: loud passages get a large step to avoid overload, quiet ones a small step to cut granular noise.
   - Answer.
*/
