/* book Worked example: Q: A signal has σ² = 1 V² and adjacent-sample correlation ρ = 0.9. Find the prediction-error variance and the prediction gain for x̂[n] = x̃[n − 1] and for the best coefficient a = ρ. About how many bits does DPCM save?
   1. Simple predictor: σe² = 2σ²(1 − ρ) = 2 × 1 × 0.1 = 0.2 V².
   2. Gp = 10 log10(1/0.2) = 10 log10 5 = 6.99 dB.
   3. Best coefficient: σe² = σ²(1 − ρ²) = 1 − 0.81 = 0.19 V², so Gp = 10 log10(1/0.19) = 7.21 dB.
   4. Bits saved ≈ Gp/6.02 = 6.99/6.02 = 1.16, so about one bit per sample.
   Answer: σe² = 0.2 V² and Gp = 6.99 dB (0.19 V² and 7.21 dB with a = 0.9); DPCM saves about one bit per sample, 8 kbit/s at 8 kHz. */
