/* book Worked example: Q: A 1 kHz sinusoid of peak 2 V is delta modulated with Δ = 0.1 V and fs = 32 kHz. (a) Find the largest slope of the input and of the staircase. (b) Does slope overload occur? (c) Find the smallest fs, and the smallest Δ at 32 kHz, that avoid it.
   1. Input: 2πfA = 2π × 1000 × 2 = 12 566 V/s.
   2. Staircase: Δfs = 0.1 × 32 000 = 3200 V/s.
   3. 12 566 > 3200 V/s, so slope overload occurs near every zero crossing.
   4. Smallest fs = 2πfA/Δ = 12 566/0.1 = 125 664 samples/s, about 125.7 kHz.
   5. Or keep 32 kHz and raise the step: Δ ≥ 12 566/32 000 = 0.393 V, at the cost of more granular noise.
   Answer: Slope overload occurs (12 566 V/s against 3200 V/s); avoid it with fs ≥ 125.7 kHz, or Δ ≥ 0.393 V at 32 kHz. */
